const __vite_ssr_export_default__ = /* #__PURE__ */ JSON.parse("[\n  {\n    \"day\": 7,\n    \"title\": \"Ship Dukaan v1.0\",\n    \"intro\": \"Six days of pieces: display, bills, decisions, loops, functions, lists, files. Today they become one thing a stranger can use. v1.0 adds no new ideas - it is finishing: a menu that never crashes, a README a stranger can follow, and a demo to a real person. This is Project 1, the checkpoint for all of Phase 1.\",\n    \"objectives\": [\n      \"Assemble the week's pieces into one menu-driven program.\",\n      \"Write a README that lets a stranger run the tool without your help.\",\n      \"Demo the tool to a real person and fix what confuses them.\"\n    ],\n    \"concepts\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Done means a stranger can use it\",\n        \"text\": \"Your program works for you because you know how it wants to be used. Done means Amina's cousin can run it from your README alone: what the program does, how to start it, what each menu choice does. Scope is a design decision - v1.0 does five things: view stock, add item, print a bill, save, quit. Nothing more. Every extra feature is a new way to be broken; ship the five working.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"One complete path beats five half-paths\",\n        \"text\": \"Pick \\\"add an item\\\" and make it work end to end: menu choice, ask for name and numbers, reject bad input with a message, save the file, confirm on screen. That is a vertical slice - one thin path through the whole program, finished. Then do the next. Five half-built features demo as five broken promises; two finished paths demo as a tool.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"The demo is part of the project\",\n        \"text\": \"Watching a real person use your program finds what testing misses: they press 6 when the menu has 5 choices, they type the item name where the price goes. Their confusion is the bug report. A menu that never crashes is built from pieces you already 